WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.090
Jon Bentley once asked professional

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.990
programmers to write binary search,

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.530
about a dozen lines of code.

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.700
Around ninety percent of

5
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.640
them had bugs in it.

6
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.940
Off-by-one mistakes at the edges

7
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.300
are a common kind of bug.

8
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.100
Today: testing like an engineer.

9
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.440
A unit test runs one small piece of

10
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.960
code and checks the answer.

11
00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.150
In Python, assert stops the

12
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.070
program if its check is false.

13
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.950
This function passes the first test.

14
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.650
But the second fails: best

15
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.870
starts at zero, which is bigger

16
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.510
than every negative number.

17
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.790
Ordinary data rarely breaks

18
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.790
code; the edges do.

19
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.300
Test an empty list and a list of one.

20
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.120
Test the first and the last position.

21
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.050
Test values that repeat, negatives, and

22
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.810
something that isn’t there at all.

23
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.810
Here’s the outline of a binary

24
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.790
search that passes a test in the

25
00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:06.990
middle of a long list.

26
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.270
But search a one-item list for its only

27
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.590
item, and it says minus one: not found.

28
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.000
The loop must run while lo is less

29
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.720
than or equal to hi, or it skips

30
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.880
the last place to look.

31
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.100
Four short tests catch it.

32
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.760
An empty list, one item, the last

33
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.260
item, and a value that isn’t there.

34
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.250
Run them after every change, and a

35
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.970
boundary bug can’t sneak back in.

36
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:36.610
Quick check!

37
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.810
A function finds the

38
00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.230
largest mark in a list.

39
00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.990
Which test is most likely to catch a bug?

40
00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.690
The list of only negative

41
00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.250
numbers: an edge case.

42
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.160
Five ordinary marks would pass

43
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.600
even the buggy version.

44
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.640
Write the tests that try to break your

45
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.660
code, not the ones that flatter it.

46
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.240
Engineers often write the test first.

47
00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.730
Red: write a test, and watch it fail.

48
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.440
Green: write the simplest

49
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.520
code that passes.

50
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.820
Refactor: tidy the code while

51
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.280
the tests still pass.

52
00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.930
Then the tests are a safety net

53
00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.350
for every later change.

54
00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.350
Tests can’t catch everything.

55
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.770
A second pair of eyes spots

56
00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.170
unclear names and missing cases.

57
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.730
Keep each change small, say why you

58
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.630
made it, and review kindly: comment

59
00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.590
on the code, not the coder.

60
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.050
In the app, open Guess the number.

61
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.300
Find a hidden number from one to a

62
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.060
thousand by halving, and count your

63
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:43.320
guesses.

64
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.820
Then test the edges: can halving

65
00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.100
find one, and a thousand?

66
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:50.990
Your turn.

67
00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.490
You’re working test-first

68
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.790
on a new function.

69
00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.170
What do you write before the code?

70
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.540
A test that fails.

71
00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.650
In short: unit tests check

72
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.690
small pieces with assert.

73
00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.610
Test the edges: empty, one item,

74
00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.850
first and last, not there.

75
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.610
Work test-first: red, green, refactor.

76
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.560
And review code with others.

77
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.600
Try it on your own code: take a function

78
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.720
you wrote this term, and write five

79
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.380
asserts for it, at least three at the

80
00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:27.560
edges.

81
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:28.850
Run them.

82
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.740
If one fails, you’ve found a bug

83
00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.220
before anyone else did.
